This file basically used to explain how django admin user created and static files are loaded.

1. To load the static files.
    Add the below code in project(MYBlogs) settings.py.
    STATIC_URL = '/static/'

    STATIC_ROOT = BASE_DIR / 'staticfiles'

    STATICFILES_DIRS = [
        BASE_DIR / "static",
    ]

    Then run the below command:
    python manage.py collectstatic

    This will create the staticfiles folder in project level and under that we hv css, img, jss files/folder.

2. To create the admin user:
    Run : python manage.py createsuperuser
    So it will prompt for username, email and password.

3. To login to admin page
    Make user /admin was defined in urls.pattern under project folder.
    then hit url : localhost:8000/admin

4. To create the data in sql lite database and to acess below is the step by step process, here we just create the title, author and description for blog table and we designed html so that in blog page it will just list the blogs, when we click on specific blog, it will redirected detailed blog page.

Here are the step by step process.
STEP 1: Create Model (Database Table)
    Go to:
    blogs/models.py

    Add:
    from django.db import models
    class Blog(models.Model):
        title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
        author = models.CharField(max_length=100)
        description = models.TextField()
        created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)

        def __str__(self):
            return self.title

    What happens here?

    Django will create SQLite table:
    blogs_blog

    Columns:
    id (auto created primary key ✅)
    title
    author
    description
    created_at

STEP 2: Apply Migrations
    Run:
    python manage.py makemigrations
    python manage.py migrate

    This creates table in SQLite DB

STEP 3: Enable Admin
    Go to:
    blogs/admin.py

    Add:
    from django.contrib import admin
    from .models import Blog
    admin.site.register(Blog)

STEP 4: Create Admin User
    python manage.py createsuperuser
    Enter:
    username
    password

STEP 5: Run Server
    python manage.py runserver
    Open:
    http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/
    Login → You will see Blogs
    Click → Add blog data 🎉

STEP 6: Create Views (Frontend Logic)
    Go to:
    blogs/views.py
    from django.shortcuts import render, get_object_or_404
    from .models import Blog

    def blog_list(request):
        blogs = Blog.objects.all()
        return render(request, 'blogs/blog_list.html', {'blogs': blogs})

    def blog_detail(request, id):
        blog = get_object_or_404(Blog, id=id)
        return render(request, 'blogs/blog_detail.html', {'blog': blog})

STEP 7: URLs
    blogs/urls.py
        from django.urls import path
        from . import views

        urlpatterns = [
            path('', views.blog_list, name='blog_list'),
            path('<int:id>/', views.blog_detail, name='blog_detail'),
        ]

    MYBlogs/urls.py
        from django.contrib import admin
        from django.urls import path, include

        urlpatterns = [
            path('admin/', admin.site.urls),
            path('blogs/', include('blogs.urls')),
        ]

STEP 8: Templates
    Create folder:
        blogs/templates/blogs/
            blog_list.html:
            <h1>Blogs</h1>

            <ul>
                {% for blog in blogs %}
                    <li>
                        <a href="{{ blog.id }}/">
                            {{ blog.title }}
                        </a>
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>

            blog_detail.html:
            <h1>{{ blog.title }}</h1>
            <p><b>Author:</b> {{ blog.author }}</p>
            <p>{{ blog.description }}</p>
            <p><i>{{ blog.created_at }}</i></p>

    FINAL RESULT
    Open:
    http://127.0.0.1:8000/blogs/
    You’ll see:
    List of blogs

    Click one → goes to:

    /blogs/1/
    Shows:
    Title
    Author
    Description
    Created date


Important Concepts You Learned
Flow:
Model → Migration → DB (SQLite)
       ↓
Admin → Add Data
       ↓
View → Fetch Data
       ↓
Template → Show UI
       ↓
URL → Navigation